<http://data.silknow.org/object/7080f870-215d-3c7d-957a-a7dc3a685a9e/observation/1>
        a                   crmsci:S4_Observation ;
        ecrm:P2_has_type    <http://data.silknow.org/observation/technical-observation> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note    "Woman's petticoat of pale blue silk satin lined with glazed undyed wool and wadded with a thin layer of wool.  It is quilted in running stitch with  pale  blue silk thread in a horizontal pattern of an undulating leafy vine around the hem, with 10 rows of ogee arches above. The petticoat is made  of 6 panels of silk, with pocket openings cut into the side panels and bound with ¾ inch wide (1.8 cm) pale blue silk ribbon.  The same ribbon binds  the hem.  There are casings with ties on either side to adjust the width over hoops.\n\nThe petticoat was altered in the late 19th century, probably for fancy dress. The waist binding and ties for the back of the petticoat were removed  and those for the front carried to the back and stitched down."@en ;
        crmsci:O8_observed  <http://data.silknow.org/object/7080f870-215d-3c7d-957a-a7dc3a685a9e> .

